# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever clones it.
#
# Without this, git's behaviour depends on each person's core.autocrlf setting,
# and a colleague with a different one turns every file in the repository into a
# diff the first time they save anything. Worse, it can rewrite the line endings
# inside the .traineddata and .ttf files, which corrupts them silently - the OCR
# then reads nothing and the Hindi font draws boxes, with no error anywhere.

# Text files: one line ending in the repository, whatever the working copy uses.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch and command files must keep CRLF or cmd.exe mis-parses them.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The hand-over notes are read in Notepad on a department PC. LF alone used to
# render as one long line there; modern Notepad copes, but CRLF costs nothing
# and these are the files most likely to be opened by somebody who is not a
# programmer.
packaging/*.txt text eol=crlf

# Binary. Never touched, never diffed, never line-ending-converted.
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.ttc binary
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.zip binary
*.traineddata binary

# Keeps GitHub's language bar describing the project rather than its fonts.
clippings_manager/assets/** linguist-vendored
NOTES.md linguist-documentation
